.TH CAPTURE_CDP_LLDP 1 "2026-06-03" "xdp" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
capture_cdp_lldp \- capture and decode the first CDP/LLDP frame on an interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B capture_cdp_lldp
.RI [ interface ]
.br
.B capture_cdp_lldp
.B \-s
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B capture_cdp_lldp
listens on a network interface for the first CDP (Cisco Discovery Protocol)
or LLDP (Link Layer Discovery Protocol) advertisement, prints its full decoded
contents, and exits. The decoded output reveals switch and port information
such as the neighbor's system name, port ID, and native VLAN.
.PP
These frames are typically broadcast every 30\(en60 seconds, so it may take up
to a minute before any output appears.
.PP
If no interface is given, the first connected (carrier present), non-loopback
interface is selected automatically. Packet capture usually requires elevated
privileges; if the interface cannot be opened as the current user, the command
re-runs itself with
.BR sudo (8).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I interface
Capture on the named interface (for example
.IR eth0 ).
.TP
.B \-s
Interactively select the interface from a menu of active, non-loopback
interfaces.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success. Returns a non-zero status if no suitable interface is
found or if the underlying capture fails.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B capture_cdp_lldp
Auto-pick the first connected, non-loopback interface.
.TP
.B capture_cdp_lldp \-s
Choose an interface interactively.
.TP
.B capture_cdp_lldp eth0
Capture on
.IR eth0 .
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.BR tshark (1)
(Wireshark CLI) and the
.B iproute2
suite
.RB ( ip (8)).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tshark (1),
.BR ip (8),
.BR sudo (8)
